@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "YAML vs Classic Pipelines";
    ViewData["ActiveMenu"] = "AzureDevOps";
}

<div class="container py-4">
    <p class="text-center lead">Azure DevOps provides two types of pipelines: **YAML Pipelines** and **Classic Pipelines**. Understanding the differences helps teams choose the best approach for automating software builds and deployments.</p>
    <hr />

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What are YAML Pipelines?</h2>
        <p>YAML Pipelines are **code-defined pipelines** that enable teams to manage and version-control their build and deployment processes.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Defined as **.yml files** stored in the source repository.</li>
            <li>Supports **continuous integration and deployment (CI/CD)** workflows.</li>
            <li>Allows **better traceability** by storing pipeline definitions in version control.</li>
            <li>Highly flexible with support for **conditional logic and reusable templates**.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/yaml-pipeline-example.png" alt="YAML Pipeline Example" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Example of a YAML Pipeline in Azure DevOps</small></p>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Advantages of YAML Pipelines</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>**Code as Configuration:** Pipeline definitions are stored as code.</li>
                <li>**Version Control Integration:** Enables change tracking and rollback.</li>
                <li>**Reusability:** Allows templates for modular pipeline design.</li>
                <li>**Multi-Stage Support:** Supports end-to-end workflows in a single file.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>What are Classic Pipelines?</h2>
        <p>Classic Pipelines are **graphical user interface (GUI)-based pipelines** that allow users to configure builds and releases using a drag-and-drop interface.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Configured using the **Azure DevOps portal**.</li>
            <li>Utilizes a **visual editor** with predefined task templates.</li>
            <li>Supports **build and release pipelines separately**.</li>
            <li>Best suited for teams who prefer **UI-driven workflows**.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="row align-items-center">
        <div class="col-md-6">
            <h4>Advantages of Classic Pipelines</h4>
            <ul>
                <li>**User-Friendly:** No need for coding knowledge.</li>
                <li>**Drag-and-Drop Interface:** Easy to configure and modify.</li>
                <li>**Predefined Task Templates:** Simplifies setup for common tasks.</li>
                <li>**Step-by-Step Execution:** Better visibility into pipeline stages.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="col-md-6 text-center">
            <img src="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/devops/media/classic-pipeline-example.png" alt="Classic Pipeline Example" class="img-fluid rounded shadow-lg">
            <p class="text-muted mt-2"><small>Example of a Classic Pipeline in Azure DevOps</small></p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Comparison: YAML vs Classic Pipelines</h2>
        <p>The following table compares YAML and Classic Pipelines:</p>
        <table class="table table-bordered text-center">
            <thead class="table-dark">
                <tr>
                    <th>Feature</th>
                    <th>YAML Pipelines</th>
                    <th>Classic Pipelines</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Definition</strong></td>
                    <td>Code-based (YAML file stored in repo)</td>
                    <td>Graphical UI-based</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Version Control</strong></td>
                    <td>Fully integrated</td>
                    <td>Limited versioning</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Flexibility</strong></td>
                    <td>Highly flexible (supports conditions, loops, templates)</td>
                    <td>Limited flexibility</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Ease of Use</strong></td>
                    <td>Requires YAML knowledge</td>
                    <td>Beginner-friendly with drag-and-drop interface</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td><strong>Best For</strong></td>
                    <td>Complex CI/CD workflows, Infrastructure as Code (IaC)</td>
                    <td>Basic deployments, teams unfamiliar with YAML</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Choosing the Right Pipeline</h2>
        <p>Select the appropriate pipeline type based on your team's needs:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Use **YAML Pipelines** if you prefer **code-based automation, version control, and reusable templates**.</li>
            <li>Use **Classic Pipelines** if you want a **quick and visual setup** without YAML expertise.</li>
            <li>For **complex, enterprise-level workflows**, YAML Pipelines provide more flexibility.</li>
            <li>For **small teams or beginners**, Classic Pipelines offer an easier learning curve.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-5">
        <h2>Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
            YAML and Classic Pipelines serve different purposes in Azure DevOps. While YAML pipelines provide **better automation, flexibility, and version control**, Classic Pipelines offer a **simpler, UI-based configuration** for teams unfamiliar with YAML.
        </p>
        <p>
            The next step is to explore **Integrating Azure Repos & GitHub with Azure Pipelines**.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="d-flex justify-content-between mt-4">
        <a href="@Url.Action("CreatingConfiguringBuildPipelines", "AzureDevOps")" class="btn btn-outline-secondary">&larr; Previous: Creating & Configuring Build Pipelines</a>
